# UI quality bar

Always ship **best-effort UI** without waiting for the user to complain. Benchmark against **Blinkit / Zepto / Instamart / Zomato** ops and consumer patterns: clear hierarchy, dense-but-breathable lists, strong primary actions, readable on a phone.

## Mobile-first (default assumption)

**Design and review every screen as if it is used on a phone first** (buyer, vendor, delivery hub, delivery agent). Desktop is a wider layout of the same mobile UX — not the primary canvas.

- One column by default; no side-by-side list/detail unless `useIsMobile()` is false.
- List → detail drill-in on mobile (back control to return to the list).
- Compact rows/cards are good; **hit targets must stay ≥ `var(--touch-min)` (44px)** for tappable rows and primary buttons.
- Prefer short human labels over long sentences; put full copy in `title` / expanders if needed.
- Avoid multi-column “box grids” for worklists — use a **compact vertical list**.
- Sticky/primary actions near the thumb when a screen is action-heavy.
- Inputs stay ≥ 16px on mobile (already enforced globally) so iOS does not zoom.
- Respect safe-area / bottom tab bar; do not bury critical actions under chrome.

## Defaults for data screens

- Prefer **tables** for multi-field records (orders, wallet, claims, addresses) with sticky header and horizontal scroll on small screens — or compact list rows when a table is too wide on phone.
- Prefer **tabs** when a page has multiple heavy datasets (do not stack unbounded sections).
- Always **paginate** (Prev/Next or numbered) — see `scale-list-ux.mdc`.
- Short human labels; status pills; money right-aligned; tap-to-call phones.
- Toolbars wrap cleanly; search and filters stay usable with one thumb.

## Visual

- **Screen space is very valuable.** Prefer dense layouts: pair related fields in one row when width allows, shrink vertical gaps, avoid tall empty cards and padded “section boxes” that only hold 1–2 inputs. Do not stack every field full-width with large padding unless accessibility requires it.
- One clear hero (name + key metric). Avoid empty tall cards and sparse two-column imbalance.
- Consistent spacing, borders, muted meta text; no cluttered badges or competing accents.
- Loading / empty / error states that are calm and specific.
- Keep touch targets ≥ 44px for primary actions; density must not shrink hit areas below that.

## Process

If a screen looks “admin prototype” or “desktop spreadsheet”, refine it in the same pass for phone use. Do not leave “we’ll polish later” for list/detail UIs.
